# ripgrep-only overrides. Git does NOT read this file, so everything listed here
# stays gitignored and unpushable — this ONLY re-enables it for SEARCH.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------
# `company/` (.gitignore:15), `knowledge/` (:14) and `CLAUDE.md` (:23) are all
# excluded from the public repo — correctly, since company/ is the private
# business SSoT. But ripgrep honours .gitignore by default, so a search started
# at the repo root silently skipped every runbook, every requirement, every
# vendor file, every session note, AND the operating guide itself.
#
# Measured 2026-08-05:
#   rg -l "MaxAuthTries" .          -> No matches found      (FALSE NEGATIVE)
#   rg -l "MaxAuthTries" company/   -> operations.md, oracle.md
#
# A root-level "no matches" was an ABSENCE OF SEARCH, not an absence of the fact.
# That is how a world-open 3389/tcp rule on the Oracle box survived four separate
# "only 22/80/443" claims in this corpus: the two facts sat in different files, in
# different vocabularies, and no search ever brought them into contact.
#
# 🔴 RE-MEASURED 2026-08-14, AND THE EXAMPLE ABOVE IS NOW HALF FALSE — WHICH IS
#    ITSELF THE POINT, SO IT IS CORRECTED RATHER THAN DELETED.
#      rg -l "MaxAuthTries" company/  -> runbooks/operations.md, PROJECT_STATE.md
#    `oracle.md` IS NO LONGER IN company/. [ADR 054] moved the vendor set to the
#    shared brain at ../../nikatru/, a SIBLING of this repo. So the very command
#    this file offers as the REMEDY now returns an incomplete answer for the very
#    fact it was written about.
#
# 🔴 AND `.ignore` CANNOT FIX IT — for the same structural reason the `.claude/`
#    negation below could not. A negation re-includes a path that some ignore rule
#    excluded; it cannot reach OUTSIDE the search root. `!nikatru/` here would
#    match nothing, because nikatru/ is not under this directory at all.
#
# ✅ SO THE FIX IS AGAIN AN INVOCATION, NOT A RULE — SEARCH BOTH ROOTS BY NAME:
#      rg "<pattern>" . ../../nikatru/
#    Anything that greps only this repo for a BUSINESS fact — a vendor, an account
#    id, the legal name, a compliance date — is asking the wrong tree and will get
#    a clean, confident, wrong "no matches". CLAUDE.md's "Repo layout" section
#    carries the one line that says where the brain is.
#
# SAFETY: git does not read .ignore. Re-verify after any change with
#   git check-ignore -v company knowledge CLAUDE.md .claude .claude/secrets.env
# which must still report all of them as ignored.
# 🔴 REPOINTED 2026-08-15, AND IT WAS INERT FOR HOURS BEFORE ANYONE NOTICED.
# `company/` and `knowledge/` moved under `Private/` that morning. These two
# negations then matched NOTHING, while `.gitignore` gained `Private/` — so the
# whole private corpus went invisible to search again, by the same mechanism this
# file was written to defeat, in the same session that wrote the move.
#   MEASURED before the fix:
#     rg -l "PROJECT_STATE" .            -> 0 files under Private/
#     rg --no-ignore --hidden … Private  -> 81 files actually contain it
# Every measurement taken from the repo root between the move and this fix read a
# smaller corpus than it thought — including the measurements OF the move.
# 📌 A negation is a pointer at a path, and it goes silently inert the moment that
# path moves. It cannot fail loudly, because "no matches" is a valid answer.
# ⚠️ BOTH CASES, AND THE FIRST ATTEMPT FAILED WITHOUT SAYING SO. `.gitignore`
# carries `Private/` AND `private/`; the rule that actually matched was the
# LOWERCASE one (`git check-ignore` reported `.gitignore:23:private/`). A single
# `!Private/` left the search at zero and looked exactly like a fix. Windows'
# filesystem is case-insensitive; ripgrep's glob matching is not.
# ⚠️ AND THE CHILDREN NEED THEIR OWN NEGATIONS — the second failed fix.
# `.gitignore:14` (`knowledge/`) and `:15` (`company/`) are UNANCHORED, so they
# match at ANY depth: re-including `Private/` still left `Private/company/` and
# `Private/knowledge/` excluded by the rules written for their old locations.
# Two negations that each looked correct, and the search stayed at zero through
# both. Measured after each attempt, which is the only reason this is not still
# broken — "no matches" never announces itself as an absence of search.
!Private/
!private/
!Private/company/
!Private/knowledge/
!CLAUDE.md

# ── ADDED 2026-08-13, and DELIBERATELY NARROWER THAN `.claude/` ──────────────
# The same false negative recurred one directory over. `.claude/` is gitignored
# (.gitignore:13) AND hidden, and ripgrep skips hidden dot-directories EVEN UNDER
# `--no-ignore` — so only `rg --hidden --no-ignore` ever reached it. An audit of
# "what still reads the old rclone remotes" therefore returned a clean FALSE
# NEGATIVE over the very directory holding the backup scripts:
#
#   rg -c --no-ignore "gcrypt:|gdrive:" .   -> 16 files, ZERO from .claude/
#   rg -c --hidden --no-ignore "…"          -> 29 files, incl. .claude/scripts/
#                                              backup-project.ps1 (7 hits)
#
# 🔴 THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `!.claude/…` LINE HERE, FOR TWO INDEPENDENT REASONS.
#
# 1. A NEGATION CANNOT WORK AT THIS PATH — TESTED, NOT ASSUMED. `.gitignore:13`
#    excludes the DIRECTORY `.claude/`, and neither git nor ripgrep can re-include
#    a path whose parent directory is excluded. `!.claude/scripts/` was written
#    here on 2026-08-13 and MEASURED INERT: a root `rg -l "gbiz:" .` still reached
#    zero files under .claude/, with and without `--hidden`. It was removed rather
#    than left in place, because a negation that looks like it re-enabled search
#    is worse than none — the next auditor reads the line and trusts the sweep.
#    Making it work needs `!.claude/` (the whole vault) plus a DENYLIST of secret
#    files, which fails OPEN for every secret file added later. Not acceptable.
#
# 2. WE DO NOT WANT IT ANYWAY. `.claude/` is the vault: `secrets.env`, `PEM.txt`,
#    `pack-signing.seed`, `Google drive_client_secret.json`, `nikatru-platform-*.json`
#    (a GCP service-account key), `rclone-box.conf`. Re-enabling it for search puts
#    all of them on the path of any routine `rg`, and an agent's search results land
#    in a transcript.
#
# ✅ SO THE FIX IS AN INVOCATION, NOT A RULE. When auditing "what still reads X",
#    .claude/ must be swept EXPLICITLY — an explicit path defeats both the ignore
#    rules and the hidden-directory skip:
#
#      rg "gcrypt:|gdrive:" .claude/scripts/     # explicit path — always searched
#      rg --hidden --no-ignore "…" .             # or sweep everything, knowingly
#
#    Measured 2026-08-13: `rg -c --no-ignore "gcrypt:|gdrive:" .` found 16 files and
#    ZERO from .claude/, while the explicit sweep found `backup-project.ps1` with 7
#    hits. A root-level "no matches" over a HIDDEN gitignored directory is an absence
#    of search, not an absence of the fact — the same lesson as company/ above, which
#    a negation could fix and this one cannot.
